<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Ballin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Ballin (1857–1918) was a German shipping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> who directed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-america-line">Hamburg America Line</a> (HAPAG) from 1899. Under him it became the largest shipping company in the world, carrying millions of emigrants and pioneering luxury cruises. A friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, he worked for an Anglo-German naval understanding, and he died by suicide in November 1918 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> collapsed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-ballin.jpg" alt="Albert Ballin" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographerUnknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Ballin_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
